laim that GMC minimizes the exact vector bias over arbitrary sample-specific rules: equal retained mass can still lead to different cancellations and hence different biases. Rather, Propositions 1 and 2 identify GMC as the unique direction-preserving rule that retains the shared second-moment guarantee while minimizing a certified bias envelope. TIC-GRPO enforces the same mass cap by deleting individual high-ratio responses; GMC instead applies the smallest uniform attenuation needed to satisfy the cap. The next section uses this groupwise structure to derive the GMC conditional-bias and convergence bounds.

This section establishes the convergence guarantee for GMC-GRPO under mixed rollout delays. By Lemma 1 , it suffices to control the update second moment and the squared conditional bias. We first decompose the GMC update into the current-policy gradient and an explicit groupwise correction term, and show that the group-mass cap bounds the update second moment independently of the delay. We then bound the correction bias through trajectory divergences between the current and behavior policies, and relate these divergences to the parameter drift accumulated within each delay window. Combining these estimates with Lemma 1 yields the delay-explicit guarantee in Theorem 3 . A final local-overlap argument converts the chi-square branch into the group-size refinement used in the main text. Throughout, conditional statements rely on the sampling and filtration conditions in Assumption 3 . For each group, define the raw importance-sampling, self-normalized, and group-mass-capped estimators U n , b IS \displaystyle U_{n,b}^{\mathrm{IS}} : = 1 T ​ G ∑ i = 1 G ρ n ( b , i ) ∇ log π θ n ( y ( b , i ) ∣ x ( b ) ) A ^ ( b , i ) , \displaystyle:= rac{1}{TG}\sum_{i=1}^{G}ho_{n}^{(b,i)}
