Asynchronous LLM Post-Training: Group-Mass Capping and Convergence Analysis Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2610.01896v1 [cs.LG] 01 Oct 2026 Asynchronous LLM Post-Training: Group-Mass Capping and Convergence Analysis Qijia He Affiliation: The Ohio State University Ruinan Jin Affiliation: The Ohio State University Jun Luo Affiliation: The Ohio State University Shaofeng Zou Affiliation: Arizona State University Yingbin Liang Affiliation: The Ohio State University Abstract Asynchronous reinforcement learning (RL) improves the efficiency of large language model post-training but introduces stale rollouts generated by earlier policies. Theoretical understanding of how this staleness affects convergence and how to mitigate its impact remains limited. We derive a convergence bound for GRPO-style algorithms that explicitly characterizes the tradeoff between the gradient estimator’s second moment and bias. For trajectory-level importance-weighted estimators, our analysis shows that once the second moment is uniformly controlled, delay enters the bound through the bias introduced by clipping or rescaling. Guided by this insight, we propose a novel group mass capping GRPO (GMC-GRPO) method, which minimizes a ratio-based bias bound within a class of weighted estimators sharing a common second-moment guarantee. We establish convergence guarantees for asynchronous GMC-GRPO and show that, compared with TIC-GRPO, it improves the threshold dependence of the fourth-order delay term from O ⁡ ( ϵ − 4 ) O({\epsilon}^{-4}) to O ⁡ ( ϵ − 2 ) O({\epsilon}^{-2}) as ϵ → 0 {\epsilon}	o 0 , where 1 + ϵ 1+{\epsilon} is the ratio threshold. Under local policy overlap, the delay-dependent term decreases as G − 2 / 5 G^{-2/5} after tuning the step size, where G G is the group size. For fixed behavior and current policies, the bias introduced by group rescaling also vanishes as G → ∞ G	o\infty , whereas the bias from trajectory-wise clipping can persist. Experiments across Qwen3 models and reasoning benchmarks demonstrate improved robustness to stale rollouts, with GMC-GRPO achieving the best performance among stable baselines under large rollout delays. 1 introduction Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a widely used paradigm for post-training large language models (LLMs), enabling them to improve from outcome-level feedback rather than relying solely on static demonstrations ( Ouyang et al., 2022 ; Lambert et al., 2024 ) . Given a prompt, RL allows an LLM to generate thinking tokens before producing a final answer, and reinforces the generated trajectory based on its final quality ( Jaech et al., 2024 ; Guo et al., 2025 ) . Models post-trained with algorithms such as GRPO ( Shao et al., 2024 ) and its variants ( Yu et al., 2026 ; Liu et al., 2025 ; Zheng et al., 2026 ) have shown strong capabilities on challenging reasoning problems, such as math ( Cobbe et al., 2021 ; He et al., 2024 ) , coding ( Jain et al., 2025 ) , and agentic tasks ( Du et al., 2026 ) . To reduce the inefficiency of repeatedly alternating between rollout generation and policy optimization, modern RL training pipelines increasingly adopt asynchronous execution ( Zhong et al., 2024 ; Sheng et al., 2025 ) . Such systems decouple rollout generation from policy optimization: rollout workers continuously generate responses using their local policy versions, while the learner updates the policy as soon as sufficient completed rollouts have accumulated and publishes the updated parameters without blocking generation ( Fu et al., 2026 ; Hu et al., 2026 ; Noukhovitch et al., 2025 ) . Together with optimized training engines and workload scheduling, this design allows inference and training to proceed concurrently, improving resource utilization and substantially increasing the training throughput of GRPO-style methods ( Wang et al., 2025 ) . The decoupling, however, introduces data staleness . Since rollout workers and the learner progress independently, the learner may update the policy several times before a generated rollout is consumed. Consequently, a training batch can mix trajectories collected under multiple earlier policy versions, creating a mismatch between the behavior policies that generated the data and the current policy being optimized ( Fu et al., 2026 ) . As the delay grows, these trajectories can become increasingly off-policy, making policy optimization more difficult and reducing training efficiency ( Tian et al., 2026 ; Zheng et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026 ) . Despite the empirical success of delay-aware algorithms ( Roux et al., 2025 ; Xi et al., 2026 ; Huang et al., 2026 ) , how this staleness affects the convergence of GRPO-style algorithms, and how to mitigate its impact through principled algorithm design, remain poorly understood. This raises a natural but fundamental research question: Can we theoretically characterize how data staleness affects the convergence of GRPO-style algorithms, and use this to design RL algorithms better suited to asynchronous training systems? We answer this question by first identifying what any importance-weighted algorithm must control in order to converge, and then designing the correction that best meets this requirement under delay. Our key contributions are as follows: • Second moment and bias tradeoff. We derive a convergence bound for GRPO-style algorithms which explicitly captures the tradeoff between the second moment of gradient estimators and the bias error. With trajectory-level importance sampling, stale rollouts can produce large importance ratios and enlarge the gradient estimator’s second moment. Clipping these ratios controls the second moment but can introduce bias. When the resulting second-moment bound is independent of delay, delay enters the convergence bound through this clipping bias. This motivates algorithms that minimize a bias bound while maintaining a common second-moment guarantee. • GMC-GRPO. Guided by this principle, we introduce a class of weighted gradient estimators that satisfy a common second-moment bound. This class includes several GRPO-style methods as special cases. Within this class, we derive group mass capping (GMC)-GRPO by minimizing a bias bound and selecting a solution that preserves each group’s gradient direction, while retaining the same second-moment guarantee. This makes GMC-GRPO particularly well suited to asynchronous training, where its principled weighting rule effectively limits the bias. • Convergence of asynchronous GMC-GRPO. We establish a convergence guarantee for asynchronous GMC-GRPO with explicit dependence on stochastic rollout delays. Compared with TIC-GRPO ( Pang et al., 2025 ) , GMC-GRPO yields a strictly smaller delay-dependent penalty: in the tight-clipping regime, the penalty improves from O ⁡ ( ϵ − 4 ) O({\epsilon}^{-4}) to O ⁡ ( ϵ − 2 ) O({\epsilon}^{-2}) , where 1 + ϵ 1+{\epsilon} is the threshold for individual importance ratios in TIC-GRPO and their group average in GMC-GRPO. Moreover, because GMC-GRPO rescales a group only when its average importance ratio exceeds the stability cap, its correction bias vanishes as G G grows and the group average concentrates. In contrast, trajectory-wise clipping acts separately on every ratio, leaving a nonzero clipping bias even in the large-group limit. • Experiments. Finally, we evaluate GMC-GRPO under both controlled fixed delays and in a simulated asynchronous pipeline where rollout staleness is correlated with response length. Across Qwen3 models and reasoning benchmarks, GMC-GRPO achieves the best performance among vanilla GRPO-style algorithms and other baselines designed for asynchronous training. These results demonstrate effectiveness of our theory-guided design under asynchronous rollout delays. Related Work. We defer the comprehensive discussion of related work to Appendix A . Our paper is most closely related to off-policy correction for stale LLM rollouts in asynchronous RL systems ( Noukhovitch et al., 2025 ; Fu et al., 2026 ; Hu et al., 2026 ) . Typical strategies include adaptive clipping thresholds ( Roux et al., 2025 ; Xi et al., 2026 ; Zhao et al., 2026 ; Li et al., 2025 ) , variance-aware step-size control ( Zheng et al., 2026 ; Huang et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026 ) , and reuse of stale experience through staged training with selective filtering or replay buffers ( Tian et al., 2026 ; Arnal et al., 2026 ; Ritter et al., 2026 ) . Despite the empirical success of these methods, few works have studied GRPO-style algorithms from a theoretical perspective, especially under asynchronous rollout delays. Zhou et al. (2026) characterize the GRPO gradient as a U-statistic and analyze its finite-sample error and dependence on group size, while Pang et al. (2025) introduce TIC-GRPO and provide the first rigorous convergence analysis of GRPO-style methods. Nevertheless, these studies are not under asynchronous training. More recently, Xu et al. (2026) analyze asynchronous GRPO through a generic biased-gradient framework, but leave the staleness-induced bias and higher-order remainder terms as abstract quantities in the final bound. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to provide an explicit delay-dependent convergence analysis of GRPO-style algorithms in asynchronous RL systems. 2 Preliminaries 2.1 RL post-training We denote π θ \pi_{	heta} as an autoregressive language model parameterized by θ ∈ ℝ d 	heta\in\mathbb{R}^{d} . Given a prompt x x drawn from a training distribution 𝒟 \mathcal{D} over the prompt space 𝒳 \mathcal{X} , the likelihood of a response y = ( y 1 , … , y T ) y=(y_{1},\ldots,y_{T}) is π θ ​ ( y ∣ x ) = ∏ t = 1 T π θ ​ ( y t ∣ x , y < t ) \pi_{	heta}(y\mid x)=\prod_{t=1}^{T}\pi_{	heta}(y_{t}\mid x,y_{<t}) , where 𝒜 \mathcal{A} denotes the token vocabulary, y < t := ( y 1 , … , y 