ust as staleness increases, substantially outperforming vanilla GRPO and competitive with or exceeding TIC-GRPO. 5 Experiments In this section, we evaluate GMC-GRPO against several baselines: (i) vanilla GRPO ( Shao et al., 2024 ) ; (ii) GAC, which controls stale-aligned update directions through gradient projection ( Xu et al., 2026 ) ; (iii) VCPO, which combines effective-sample-size-guided step-size scaling with an off-policy minimum-variance baseline ( Huang et al., 2026 ) ; and (iv) TIC-GRPO ( Pang et al., 2025 ) . Among these methods, GAC and VCPO are designed specifically for asynchronous training. We first evaluate robustness under controlled fixed delays and then construct a simulated asynchronous system in which rollout delay is correlated with response length. Detailed parameter settings and implementation details are deferred to Appendix B.1 . We also discuss related approaches that selectively clip a fraction of importance ratios ( Fu et al., 2026 ; Li et al., 2025 ) and report an additional comparison with A-3PO ( Li et al., 2025 ) in Appendix B.2 . Controlled Fixed Delays. To isolate the effect of staleness, we evaluate GRPO, TIC-GRPO, and GMC-GRPO on Qwen3-1.7B under fixed rollout delays. Figure 2 reports sampled Pass@1 on MATH500 for delays of D = 0 , 64 , 128 D=0,64,128 learner updates; see Appendix B.1 . The full sweep over D ∈ { 0 , 32 , 64 , 128 , 256 , 512 } D\in\{0,32,64,128,256,512\} is deferred to Appendix B.2 . Without delay, all three methods attain similar final performance. At delay 64, GMC-GRPO learns faster than TIC-GRPO and reaches comparable final Pass@1, with both outperforming vanilla GRPO. At delay 128, GMC-GRPO achieves 59.03% Pass@1, compared with 55.41% for TIC-GRPO and 35.33% for vanilla GRPO. These results show that GMC supports faster learning and stronger final performance as staleness increases. Simulated Asynchronous Delays. The fixed-delay study isolates staleness, but real rollout workers finish at different times: longer responses typically take longer to generate and thus reach the learner under older policies. To capture this coupling, we simulate an asynchronous pipeline in which rollout delay depends on generation length, producing heterogeneous delays averaging approximately 160 learner updates for Qwen3-1.7B and 140 for Qwen3-4B and Qwen3-8B. This setting tests whether GMC-GRPO remains robust when staleness varies across rollouts. Among the methods reported in Table 1 , GMC-GRPO achieves the highest mean avg@ n n scores across all three model sizes (41.47, 56.20, and 59.62) and the highest mean greedy scores on Qwen3-1.7B and Qwen3-8B (48.77 and 64.37). On Qwen3-4B, its mean greedy score of 60.63 is 0.83 points below TIC-GRPO. Notably, GMC-GRPO also outperforms the two of the most recent asynchronous-RL stabilization methods: GAC, which controls stale-aligned update directions via gradient projection, and VCPO, which combines ESS-guided step-size scaling with a minimum-variance baseline. Both improve over vanilla GRPO under delay but remain below TIC-GRPO-style trajectory correction at 4B and 8B. These results indicate that the robustness of group-mass capping is not restricted to fixed delays, but holds under heterogeneous, length-dependent staleness. Table 1: Performance under simulated asynchronous delays. Each benchmark cell is greedy/avg@ n n , with n = 16 n=16 for MATH500, Minerva, and AMC23, and n = 8 n=8 for GSM8K, Gaokao, and OlympiadBench. Avg. perf. is the average over the six benchmarks, reported as mean greedy/mean avg@ n n . Method Avg. Delay MATH500 GSM8K Gaokao Olympiad Minerva AMC23 Avg. perf. Qwen3-1.7B GRPO 158.70 48.20 / 31.04 72.18 / 47.90 39.74 / 26.27 14.69 / 6.77 16.18 / 8.32 22.50 / 9.53 35.58 / 21.64 GAC 160.02 47.00 / 31.03 72.18 / 47.09 40.26 / 25.16 15.73 / 6.70 15.81 / 8.18 20.00 / 11.09 35.16 / 21.54 VCPO 157.87 65.20 / 54.55 80.21 / 67.76 55.06 / 45.06 27.60 / 20.49 26.47 / 18.31 27.50 / 26.72 47.01 / 38.82 TIC-GRPO 159.60 62.20 / 53.61 82.34