nduce heavy-tailed importance ratios, allowing a small number of trajectories to dominate the gradient estimate and destabilize training. Learning from such data therefore requires a careful choice of how the update is corrected. Existing corrections differ mainly in where they intervene. A common strategy acts locally on individual ratios: TIC-GRPO clips a trajectory once its ratio exceeds the clipping threshold ( Pang et al., 2025 ) , while related methods replace fixed clipping with tapered weights, adaptive or asymmetric thresholds, and entropy-scaled trust regions ( Roux et al., 2025 ; Xi et al., 2026 ; Zhao et al., 2026 ; Li et al., 2025 ) . A second strategy controls batch- or update-level statistics through second-moment constraints, variance-aware step sizes, or gradient-alignment control ( Zheng et al., 2026 ; Huang et al., 2026 ; Xu et al., 2026 ) . A third changes how stale experience is reused through staged training with selective filtering, replay buffers, or objectives designed for lagged inference policies ( Tian et al., 2026 ; Arnal et al., 2026 ; Ritter et al., 2026 ) . Together, these methods show that stale data can remain informative when its effect on the update is controlled. Our work instead moves from individual correction to group-level control. We identify a cap on corrected importance mass that preserves gradient second-moment bound and derive a uniform rescaling rule that reduces delay-induced bias without selectively clipping trajectories. Theory of GRPO-Style Optimization under Delay. The convergence theory of asynchronous optimization is well developed, with rigorous and tight convergence analysis that matches the lower bound under various settings ( Joulani et al., 2013 ; Koloskova et al., 2022 ; Mishchenko et al., 2022 ; Tyurin et al., 2026 ) . In parallel, a growing line of work establishes theoretical guarantees for RL-based LLM post-training ( Xie et al., 2025 ; Huang et al., 2025b ; Foster et al., 2025 ; Huang et al., 2025a ) . Despite this progress, most existing RL-for-LLM theories are developed within the natural policy gradient and RLHF frameworks, and rigorous convergence guarantees for widely used GRPO-style algorithms remain limited, particularly under asynchronous rollout generation. Zhou et al. (2026) characterize the GRPO gradient as a U-statistic and analyze its finite-sample error and dependence on group size, while Pang et al. (2025) introduce TIC-GRPO and provide the first rigorous convergence analysis of GRPO-style methods. More recently, Xu et al. (2026) analyze asynchronous GRPO through a generic biased-gradient framework, but leave the staleness-induced bias and higher-order remainder terms as abstract quantities in the final bound. To the best of our knowledge, we are the first to provide an explicit delay-dependent convergence analysis of GRPO-style algorithms in asynchronous RL systems. Appendix B Experiment Details and supplemental Experiments B.1 Detailed Experiment Setup Models and training data. We run the experiments with the Qwen3-1.7B-Base, Qwen3-4B-Base and Qwen3-8B-Base models ( Yang et al., 2025 ) . All methods update the full actor; we use neither LoRA adapters nor an explicit KL-divergence penalty. The training set is constructed from the seven subject-specific training splits of MATH ( Hendrycks et al., 2021 ) : algebra, counting and probability, geometry, intermediate algebra, number theory, prealgebra, and precalculus. After removing examples for w
